\label{SInterKH}
Let $H$ and $K$ be subgroups of $G$. If $H_0\cup K_1$ is a perfect code in a bi-Cayley graph $\mathrm{BC}(G;R,L,S)$ over $G$, then
$S\cap KH=\emptyset$.

\label{Property}
Let $H$ and $K$ be  normal subgroups of $G$. Suppose that $S\in \mathcal{S}$, and set $C=H_0 \cup K_1$. For any $g_i\in G_0\cup G_1\setminus (S^{-1}*K)_0\cup (S*H)_1$, if $(g^2)_i\in C$, then there exists some $x_i\in C$ such that $(gx)^2=1_G$. In another words,
\begin{equation*}
  \forall g_i\in (G_0\cup G_1)\setminus ((S^{-1}*K)_0 \cup(S*H)_1),  ~~ (g^2)_i\in C~\Rightarrow~ \exists x_i\in C: ~~ (gx)^2=1_G .
\end{equation*}

\label{Perfect-Property} 
Let $H$ and $K$ be a normal subgroups of $G$.
Then, $H_0\cup K_1$ is a subgroup perfect code of $G$ if and only if the property \eqref{Property} holds.

Let $C=H_0 \cup K_1$ is a perfect code of a bi-Cayley graph $\mathrm{BC}(G;R, L, S)$ with  $S\neq \emptyset$ and $S=S^{-1}$. We let $s, s^{-1}\in S$. We obtain 
   \begin{equation}\label{s0s1}
    s_0\sim e_1\in K_1~~\text{and}~~s_1\sim e_0\in H_0.
   \end{equation}
   Take $e\neq k\in K$. Note that $(ks)_1\notin C$ because otherwise we would have $S\cap K\neq \emptyset$ which contradicts Claim \ref{SInterKH}. Therefore, $(ks)_1$ must have a neighbor in $C$. If $(ks)_1\sim k^{\prime}_1$ where $k^{\prime}\in K$, then $lks=k^{\prime}$. This leads to $s_1\sim  (k^{\prime}k^{-1})_1\in C$, contradicting \eqref{s0s1}. We now suppose that $(ks)_1\sim h_0$ where $h\in H$, then $s^{\prime}ks=h$ for some $s^{\prime}\in S$. So, we obtain $s_0\sim (hk^{-1})_1$ and because of \eqref{s0s1}, we must have $k=h\in H$. Therefore, $K\leq H$.

\label{ThCyclic}
  Let $G$ be an abelian group and $H, K\leq G$. Suppose that $P=\langle a_1 \rangle \times \dots \times \langle a_n \rangle $ is a Sylow 2-subgroup of $G$ with $o (a_i)=2^{m_i}$ for $1\leq i\leq n$.
   Let $H\cap P$ and $K\cap P$ be cyclic. Then, $H_0\cup K_1$ is a subgroup perfect code of a bi-Cayley graph $\mathrm{BC}(G; R,L, \emptyset)$ if and only if each $H\cap P$ and $K\cap P$ is either trivial or  projects onto at least one of the $\langle a_i \rangle$.
